  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "French chanson réaliste working-class-life-and-loss song genre emerging in turn-of-the-twentieth-century Parisian cabaret-and-music-hall as a Naturalist-literary parallel to the urban-realist novel of Émile Zola. Characterized by dramatically delivered ballad-form solo-vocal narratives addressing prostitution, working-class poverty, doomed romance, and street-life themes, with simple piano-or-accordion accompaniment. The mid-twentieth-century crystallization through Édith Piaf consolidated the international visibility; the parallel Damia and Fréhel lineages anchored the canonical interwar register. Distinguished from later existentialist Rive-Gauche chanson by chanson réaliste's working-class-explicit (vs intellectual-philosophical) thematic orientation.",
